Java `record` types are the natural fit for Value Objects. A `record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency)` gives you immutability, value-based `equals()` and `hashCode()`, and a clean `toString()` — all for free. The compact constructor validates invariants at creation time.

```java
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {
    public Money {
        if (amount == null || currency == null)
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount and currency required");
        amount = amount.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }

    public Money add(Money other) {
        if (!currency.equals(other.currency))
            throw new CurrencyMismatchException(currency, other.currency);
        return new Money(amount.add(other.amount), currency); // returns NEW object
    }
}
```

`add()` returns a new `Money` instance — operations produce new values rather than mutating state. This is the Value Object contract in action.

For JPA, Value Objects map to `@Embeddable` — their columns embed directly in the owning entity's row with no separate table, no join, no extra primary key. The `BankAccount` table gets `balance_amount` and `balance_currency` columns inline, not a foreign key to a `money` table. Using `@Entity` with `@Id` for Money or Address creates a pointless table, a pointless join, and an auto-generated ID that represents nothing in the domain.

The Aggregate Root is not just a container — it is the enforcement point for every business rule that spans multiple objects within the aggregate. When you call `order.addLine(productId, quantity, unitPrice)`, the `Order` root checks the order's status (can only modify draft orders), validates the quantity, creates the `OrderLine` internally, and recalculates the total. The invariant — total equals the sum of lines — is guaranteed after every mutation.

```java
public class Order {
    private final OrderId id;
    private CustomerId customerId;      // reference by ID, not object
    private final List<OrderLine> lines = new ArrayList<>();
    private OrderStatus status;
    private Money total;

    public void addLine(ProductId productId, int quantity, Money unitPrice) {
        if (status != OrderStatus.DRAFT)
            throw new OrderModificationException("Can only modify draft orders");
        lines.add(new OrderLine(productId, quantity, unitPrice));
        this.total = recalculateTotal();  // invariant maintained
    }

    public List<OrderLine> getLines() {
        return Collections.unmodifiableList(lines); // no external mutation
    }
}
```

The `getLines()` method returns an unmodifiable view — outside code can read the lines but cannot add, remove, or modify them. All mutations flow through the root's domain methods, which is what makes the invariant guarantee possible. If `OrderLine` had its own repository and could be modified independently, the total-consistency invariant would be unenforceable.

A domain event is an immutable record of something that happened, always named in **past tense** — `OrderSubmittedEvent`, not `SubmitOrderEvent`. It carries the data that handlers need to react without querying back to the source aggregate.

The aggregate root collects events in an internal list rather than publishing them directly. This keeps the domain layer free of Spring dependencies.

```java
public record OrderSubmittedEvent(
    OrderId orderId,
    CustomerId customerId,
    Money total,
    Instant occurredAt
) implements DomainEvent {}

public abstract class AggregateRoot {
    private final List<DomainEvent> domainEvents = new ArrayList<>();

    protected void registerEvent(DomainEvent event) {
        domainEvents.add(event);
    }

    public List<DomainEvent> pullDomainEvents() {
        List<DomainEvent> events = new ArrayList<>(domainEvents);
        domainEvents.clear();
        return events;
    }
}
```

The `pullDomainEvents()` method returns and clears the list atomically. This "pull" semantic ensures each event is consumed exactly once by the application service. The aggregate itself has no knowledge of Spring, Kafka, or any publishing mechanism — it simply records that something happened.

The application service orchestrates the critical sequence: load the aggregate, execute the domain method, persist the aggregate, **then** publish collected events. Getting this order wrong produces two failure modes that are notoriously difficult to debug in production.

```java
public class SubmitOrderInteractor implements SubmitOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orders;
    private final DomainEventPublisher eventPublisher;

    @Override
    public SubmitOrderResponse execute(SubmitOrderRequest request) {
        Order order = orders.findById(new OrderId(request.orderId()))
            .orElseThrow(() -> new OrderNotFoundException(request.orderId()));

        order.submit();                                    // domain logic + event collection
        orders.add(order);                                 // persist FIRST
        order.pullDomainEvents().forEach(eventPublisher::publish); // publish SECOND

        return new SubmitOrderResponse(order.getId().value());
    }
}
```

If you publish first and the save fails, downstream systems react to something that never actually happened — inventory gets reserved for an order that does not exist. If you skip the event entirely when the save succeeds, downstream systems never learn about the change. The save-then-publish ordering guarantees that published events correspond to persisted state.

For in-process event handling within a single Bounded Context, Spring's `ApplicationEventPublisher` works well — handlers annotated with `@EventListener` or `@TransactionalEventListener` react within the same application. The `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` variant is especially useful because it fires only after the database transaction commits successfully, preventing handlers from processing events for rolled-back transactions.

The repository interface lives in the domain package and uses collection-oriented naming. Methods called `add()` and `remove()` reinforce the mental model of a collection, not a database. Query methods are named after domain concepts, not database columns.

```java
public interface OrderRepository {
    void add(Order order);                              // not 'save'
    void remove(OrderId orderId);                       // not 'delete'
    Optional<Order> findById(OrderId orderId);
    List<Order> findSubmittedOrdersAwaitingPayment();   // domain concept
    boolean existsById(OrderId orderId);
}
```

The method `findSubmittedOrdersAwaitingPayment()` reads like a sentence from the Ubiquitous Language. A domain expert could look at this interface and understand what the system does with orders. Compare this to `findByStatusAndPaymentStatusIn(OrderStatus status, List<PaymentStatus> statuses)` — that method exposes the fact that "submitted and awaiting payment" is modeled as two separate status columns, which is a persistence decision the domain should not know about.

The application service uses the repository as if it were a simple collection — no awareness of transactions, connection pools, or query optimization. This is what makes use cases independently testable: swap the JPA implementation for an `InMemoryOrderRepository` backed by a `HashMap`, and the use case tests run in milliseconds with no Spring context.

The JPA implementation lives in the infrastructure package and implements the domain interface. It bridges between the domain's collection metaphor and JPA's persistence reality.

```java
@Component
public class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final OrderJpaRepository springData; // Spring Data interface
    private final OrderMapper mapper;

    @Override
    public void add(Order order) {
        springData.save(mapper.toJpaEntity(order)); // domain → JPA entity
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Order> findById(OrderId orderId) {
        return springData.findById(orderId.value())
            .map(mapper::toDomainAggregate);        // JPA entity → domain
    }

    @Override
    public List<Order> findSubmittedOrdersAwaitingPayment() {
        return springData.findByStatusAndPaymentStatus(
            OrderStatus.SUBMITTED, PaymentStatus.PENDING
        ).stream().map(mapper::toDomainAggregate).toList();
    }
}
```

The mapper translates between the domain aggregate (pure Java, no annotations) and the JPA entity (with `@Entity`, `@Id`, `@Embeddable`). This separation means the domain model is never constrained by JPA's requirements — no no-arg constructor mandates, no mutable setters for Hibernate proxies, no `@Column` annotations on domain fields.

The "collection-oriented" repository style shown here contrasts with the "persistence-oriented" style where changes are tracked automatically (like JPA's managed entities with dirty checking). Collection-oriented repositories are explicit — you `add()` when you want to persist, and the repository does not track changes between calls. This explicitness makes the code easier to reason about, especially when debugging persistence issues.

A Domain Service fills a specific gap: business logic that **involves multiple aggregates or requires domain knowledge but does not naturally fit on a single entity**. If you find yourself writing a method on an entity that requires another aggregate as a parameter — `order.applyLoyaltyDiscount(customer)` — that forces `Order` to know about `Customer`, violating aggregate isolation. Instead, create a `PricingDomainService` that accepts both.

```java
// Domain Service — pure business logic, no Spring
public class FundsTransferDomainService {
    private final TransferLimitPolicy limitPolicy;

    public TransferResult transfer(Account source, Account target, Money amount) {
        limitPolicy.validate(source, amount);  // business rule: daily limit check
        source.debit(amount);                   // each aggregate enforces its own invariants
        target.credit(amount);
        return new TransferResult(source.getId(), target.getId(), amount);
    }
}

// Application Service — orchestration only
public class TransferFundsInteractor implements TransferFundsUseCase {
    private final AccountRepository accounts;
    private final FundsTransferDomainService transferService;
    private final DomainEventPublisher eventPublisher;

    @Override
    public TransferResponse execute(TransferRequest request) {
        Account source = accounts.findById(new AccountId(request.sourceId()))
            .orElseThrow();
        Account target = accounts.findById(new AccountId(request.targetId()))
            .orElseThrow();

        TransferResult result = transferService.transfer(
            source, target, new Money(request.amount(), request.currency()));

        accounts.add(source);
        accounts.add(target);
        source.pullDomainEvents().forEach(eventPublisher::publish);
        return new TransferResponse(result);
    }
}
```

Notice the Application Service does nothing but load, delegate, save, and publish. All business decisions — limit checking, debiting, crediting — live in the Domain Service and the aggregates themselves. If you see `if/else` business rules in an Application Service, that logic probably belongs deeper. The Domain Service has no `@Component`, no `@Transactional`, and no repository calls — it is created as a `@Bean` in a `@Configuration` class with its policy dependency injected through the constructor.

Establishing the language is the beginning — maintaining it through months of development is harder. When a domain expert says "we stopped calling it a Policy — we now call it a Coverage," the code must change. This is not a cosmetic refactor; it is capturing a deeper understanding of the domain.

```java
// BEFORE: developer-invented vocabulary
public class PolicyService {
    public void processRecord(Long policyId, int typeCode, BigDecimal amt) { }
}

// AFTER: Ubiquitous Language reflected in code
public class InsuranceClaimService {
    public ClaimDecision assessClaim(PolicyId policyId, ClaimType type, Money amount) { }
}
```

The rename propagates through class names, method signatures, variable names, database columns (via migration), and API fields (with versioning). IDE refactoring tools make this safe, and the cost is far lower than the ongoing translation tax of mismatched vocabulary.

A practical enforcement mechanism is ArchUnit: `classes().that().resideInAPackage("..claims..").should().haveSimpleNameContaining("Claim")` catches classes that introduce non-standard vocabulary into the claims context. This is not foolproof, but it makes deviations visible during the build.

An ACL has three collaborating pieces. The **Port** is a domain-defined interface that expresses what the domain needs in its own language — `PaymentGateway` with methods that accept `Money`, `CustomerId`, and return `PaymentResult`. The domain layer owns this interface and knows nothing about which external system implements it.

The **Adapter** implements the port and bridges to the external system's client library. It is the only place in the codebase that imports `com.stripe.model.*` or `com.salesforce.api.*`. The adapter calls the external API, handles authentication, retries, and error mapping — all external-system-specific concerns.

The **Translator** (sometimes a separate class, sometimes methods within the adapter) converts between your domain types and the external system's types. It maps your `Money` to Stripe's cent-based `Long amount`, your `PaymentStatus.SUCCEEDED` to Stripe's `"succeeded"` string, and Stripe's `StripeException` to your domain's `PaymentGatewayException`.

```java
// Port — domain-defined interface
public interface PaymentGateway {
    PaymentResult charge(CustomerId customerId, Money amount, PaymentMethod method);
}

// Adapter + Translator — infrastructure implementation
@Component
public class StripePaymentGatewayAdapter implements PaymentGateway {
    private final StripeClient stripe;

    @Override
    public PaymentResult charge(CustomerId customerId, Money amount, PaymentMethod method) {
        try {
            PaymentIntent intent = stripe.create(translateToStripeParams(amount, method));
            return translateToPaymentResult(intent); // Stripe → domain types
        } catch (StripeException e) {
            throw new PaymentGatewayException("Payment failed", e); // Stripe → domain exception
        }
    }
}
```

The domain use case calls `paymentGateway.charge()` with domain types. It never sees `PaymentIntent`, `StripeException`, or any Stripe-specific concept.

The aggregate root and its internal entities are plain Java classes. No `@Entity`, no `@Component`, no Spring imports. The aggregate generates its own identity at creation time using `UUID.randomUUID()`, so it has a stable ID before it ever touches the database.

```java
public class Reservation extends AggregateRoot {
    private final ReservationId id;
    private final GuestId guestId;         // reference by ID, not object
    private final RoomNumber roomNumber;   // Value Object
    private DateRange stayPeriod;          // Value Object
    private ReservationStatus status;

    public static Reservation create(GuestId guestId, RoomNumber room, DateRange period) {
        Reservation r = new Reservation(
            ReservationId.generate(), guestId, room, period, ReservationStatus.CONFIRMED);
        r.registerEvent(new ReservationCreatedEvent(r.id, guestId, period));
        return r;
    }

    public void cancel(CancellationReason reason) {
        if (status == ReservationStatus.CHECKED_IN)
            throw new CannotCancelCheckedInReservationException();
        this.status = ReservationStatus.CANCELLED;
        registerEvent(new ReservationCancelledEvent(id, reason));
    }
}

public record DateRange(LocalDate checkIn, LocalDate checkOut) {
    public DateRange {
        if (!checkOut.isAfter(checkIn)) throw new InvalidDateRangeException();
    }
    public long nights() { return ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(checkIn, checkOut); }
}
```

Notice no JPA annotations, no no-arg constructor, no setters. The factory method `create()` encapsulates construction logic and fires the creation event. Value Objects like `DateRange` are records with validation in the compact constructor. This aggregate compiles with nothing but the JDK.

The domain defines what it needs; the infrastructure decides how to provide it. The repository interface lives in the domain package, and the JPA adapter lives in the infrastructure package with a separate JPA entity class.

```java
// domain/repository — no Spring imports
public interface ReservationRepository {
    void add(Reservation reservation);
    Optional<Reservation> findById(ReservationId id);
    List<Reservation> findActiveByRoom(RoomNumber room, DateRange period);
}

// infrastructure/persistence — JPA entity (separate from domain aggregate)
@Entity @Table(name = "reservations")
public class ReservationJpaEntity {
    @Id private String id;
    private String guestId;
    private String roomNumber;
    @Embedded private DateRangeEmbeddable stayPeriod;
    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) private ReservationStatus status;
}

// infrastructure/persistence — adapter bridges domain ↔ JPA
@Component
public class JpaReservationRepository implements ReservationRepository {
    private final ReservationSpringDataRepo jpa;
    private final ReservationMapper mapper;

    @Override
    public void add(Reservation reservation) {
        jpa.save(mapper.toJpa(reservation));
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Reservation> findById(ReservationId id) {
        return jpa.findById(id.value()).map(mapper::toDomain);
    }
}
```

The mapper translates between the domain aggregate (immutable, no JPA annotations) and the JPA entity (mutable, annotated, JPA-friendly). This separation means the domain model is never constrained by Hibernate's requirements. The domain tests use an `InMemoryReservationRepository` backed by a `HashMap` — no Spring context, no database.

Domain services and use cases are plain Java classes created as `@Bean` definitions in a `@Configuration` class. This makes the wiring explicit and visible — you can see exactly what depends on what.

```java
@Configuration
public class ReservationUseCaseConfig {

    @Bean
    public RoomAvailabilityService roomAvailabilityService(
            ReservationRepository repository) {
        return new RoomAvailabilityService(repository); // domain service, plain Java
    }

    @Bean
    public MakeReservationUseCase makeReservationUseCase(
            ReservationRepository repository,
            RoomAvailabilityService availabilityService,
            DomainEventPublisher eventPublisher) {
        return new MakeReservationInteractor(
            repository, availabilityService, eventPublisher);
    }
}
```

Spring auto-discovers `JpaReservationRepository` (it has `@Component`) and injects it wherever `ReservationRepository` is needed — the `@Configuration` class wires the domain service and use case with their ports. The use case class itself has no Spring annotations, no `@Service`, no `@Autowired`. It is testable by constructing it directly with mock dependencies.

The controller layer is where Spring MVC takes over. Controllers inject use case interfaces, translate HTTP request bodies to use case request objects, call the use case, and map responses back to HTTP. The controller knows HTTP; the use case knows the domain. The full chain — HTTP to domain and back — flows through clean interfaces at each boundary.

A production warning: placing `@Transactional` correctly matters. It belongs on the repository adapter's write methods or on a transactional aspect around the use case — never on the domain service or aggregate. The transaction boundary is an infrastructure concern. If `@Transactional` appears on a domain class, you have leaked Spring into the domain.
